          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Jack:</strong> In general, the world has become a place where most people communicate in short sound bites limited by word counts via social media. It is easier to communicate via text than to engage in conversation. Verbal communication styles have subsequently had to adapt to meet these restrictions by being clear and concise to get a message over quickly.</p>
<p>However, some messages are more complicated and cannot be communicated in that way but can still be explained clearly and concisely. They also must be interesting enough to engage the listener and informative enough to enable the listener to understand.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> One way of engaging listeners is to take a storytelling approach to put a message into context. Learning to express information as a story can help to draw the listeners in by having a beginning a middle and an end, rather than just expressing a list of facts and information. Even introducing characters and scenarios can make it more interesting.</p>
<p>Using metaphors and analogies can also help put across complex messages that may not be easy to explain. They can also link it to something that the listener may be more accustomed</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> to.</p>
<p>Using visual aids can also help with presentations to demonstrate ideas, give examples and reinforce information.</p>
<p>A clear and easy to understand speaking voice can help engage listeners and keep them actively listening, especially knowing when to change tone and inflection and pausing for reactions.</p>
<p>One of the</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> most important things when presenting information and ideas is to be prepared and plan what is to be communicated verbally, whether this is by use of cue cards or visual presentation. Otherwise, important information may be omitted or misrepresented with potentially damaging effects.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
